# Ruby on Rails Backend Conventions

Stack: Rails 7.x API-only, Puma, PostgreSQL via ActiveRecord (complex reads may use `exec_query` in models or query objects), migrations in `db/migrate/` guarded by `strong_migrations`, ActiveJob with Sidekiq, `ActiveModel::Serializer` or plain `as_json` (one serializer per exposed model), Rails credentials for secrets, RSpec + FactoryBot, RuboCop (also the formatter) with `rubocop-rails` and `rubocop-rspec`.

## Layout

- `app/controllers/api/v1/`, `models/`, `services/` (one service object per operation, grouped by domain: `services/jobs/score_match.rb`), `clients/` (one wrapper per external provider), `queries/` (complex or multi-model reads), `serializers/`, `jobs/` (thin, delegate to services), `mailers/`. `spec/` mirrors `app/`; specs never sit beside their source.
- Never rename or move the Rails framework directories. `lib/` is for tasks and generators; domain logic goes in `app/services/`.
- Controllers: strong params, call a service or model, render a serializer. No business logic, SQL or multi-step orchestration.
- Services: business logic across models, queries and clients; no request or response objects.
- Models: persistence, validations, scopes, associations. They never call services. No repository layer; when a read outgrows a scope, make a query object.
- Flow is `controllers -> services -> models/queries` and `services -> clients`.

## Naming and file layout

- `snake_case.rb` files and directories matching the class (`score_match.rb` defines `Jobs::ScoreMatch`). One class per file, path mirrors namespace.
- Service objects are `Verb + Noun` with a single public `call`: `Jobs::ScoreMatch.call(job:)`.
- Predicates end in `?` (`expired?`), never `is_expired`. Bang methods only for raising variants.
- Constants `UPPER_SNAKE` at the top of the class; a single-use literal stays beside its consumer.
- File order: `# frozen_string_literal: true`, header comment (what and why), class with constants, public interface (`call` for services), then `private` helpers with caller above callee.

## Controllers and errors

```ruby
class Api::V1::JobsController < ApplicationController
  def create
    result = Jobs::CreateJob.call(attributes: job_params)
    render json: JobSerializer.new(result), status: :created
  end

  private

  def job_params
    params.require(:job).permit(:title, :company, :url)
  end
end
```

- Always strong params; never pass raw `params` down.
- `rescue_from` on `ApplicationController` maps domain errors centrally (`RecordNotFound` to 404, `RecordInvalid` to 422). Rescue locally only to add a useful message. Never `rescue Exception`; rescue the narrowest class. No internals in response bodies.
- Validate at the edge: strong params for shape, model validations for domain invariants.

## Migrations

- Constant defaults are bare literals (`default: "active"`). SQL expressions are lambdas (`default: -> { "now()" }`, `default: -> { "gen_random_uuid()" }`). Never nested quotes (`"'active'"`) and never a SQL call as a bare string.
- Risky changes follow additive, backfill, switch, cleanup; never a destructive one-shot against production.

## Config and security

- Secrets live in Rails credentials or ENV, never in code or logs. An initializer fails fast on missing ENV: `raise "missing ENV #{key}" if ENV[key].blank?`.
- Parse `CORS_ORIGIN` in that initializer in every environment: strip it, return `""` for blank (no cross-origin callers), reject `*`, `null`, lists, paths and userinfo, and match `scheme://host[:port]` with `\A...\z` anchors (Ruby `^`/`$` match at every line break). Raise in production when it is blank. `rack-cors` uses `credentials: true`, so a wildcard hands the session cookie to any site.
- Session cookie: `httponly: true`, `secure: !Rails.env.development?` (not a production check, which leaves staging on plain HTTP), `same_site: :lax`.

## Containers

- The Rails app, its worker and scheduled jobs ship a Dockerfile in the commit that creates them. Keep the Rails-generated multi-stage file (`ruby:3.3-slim`, `BUNDLE_DEPLOYMENT=1`, assets precompiled in the builder, `USER rails`), pin the base tag and add `HEALTHCHECK` on `/health` for web. Worker image is `Dockerfile.worker` with `CMD ["bundle", "exec", "sidekiq"]`.
- `.dockerignore` excludes `.git`, `.env*`, `log/`, `tmp/`, `spec/`, `node_modules`. `RAILS_MASTER_KEY` and secrets arrive as run-time environment variables.
- `docker-compose.yml` runs web, worker, Postgres and Redis.

## Testing (RSpec)

- Request specs over controller specs; assert status, body shape and database effects, not mock-call counts.
- Real test database with transactional cleanup; never mock ActiveRecord. FactoryBot factories in `spec/factories/`, traits over duplicated factories.
- One negative-input spec per endpoint (oversized payload, injection attempt, malformed encoding). A security control gets a spec feeding it the insecure value (staging cookie must be Secure; unsafe `CORS_ORIGIN` raises).
- LLM consumers get one fixture spec against a real captured response. No `skip` or `pending` to hide a failing spec.
- Run with `parallel_tests` (`bundle exec parallel_rspec`), one database per process (`TEST_ENV_NUMBER`). Locally run only the affected specs; CI runs everything.
- Run `bundle exec rubocop -a` on changed files.
